      "question": {
        ":question/id": "1f635a41-44c7-5ec5-865a-04e966fdd991",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "If $f(x)=\\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{10 x^{2}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the original quotient is defined.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $v(x)=10 x^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (10 x^{2})-(2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (20 x)}{(10 x^{2})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{- 20 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 40 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{100x^4}\\\\ &=\\frac{\\cancel{10x}(- 2 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})}{\\cancel{10x}\\cdot 10x^3}\\\\ &=\\frac{- 2 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{10x^3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
